---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro'
---

<BaseLayout
  currentPath="/about/"
  title="About TRACED | Jonathan R. Reed"
  description="Why I built TRACED, where the breach data comes from, and how the local password check works without accounts or tracking."
>

  <main id="main-content" tabindex="-1" class="page">
    <header class="page-head band">
      <h1>About TRACED</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        TRACED started as a weekend project. I wanted a simpler way to browse the
        <a href="https://haveibeenpwned.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Have I Been Pwned<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>
        dataset, with one searchable list and a case file for every breach.
      </p>
      <p class="byline">
        <img
          src="https://img.jonathanrreed.com/personal/jonathan.avif"
          alt=""
          width="48"
          height="48"
          class="byline-photo"
          onerror="this.style.display='none'"
        />
        <span>
          By <a href="https://jonathanrreed.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jonathan R. Reed<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>
        </span>
      </p>
    </header>
    <div class="wrap about">

      <div class="prose">
        <section>
          <h2>What this is</h2>
          <p>
            Every breach in Have I Been Pwned gets its own case file here with the date it happened, how many accounts
            were affected, what kind of data leaked, and when HIBP added it to the database.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you want to check whether your own email address was in a breach, go straight to HIBP. I built this so
            you can actually read through the public breach history and look up old sites or apps you've used.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>How to read it</h2>
          <dl class="how">
            <div>
              <dt><span class="leak-hot">*</span>Marked data types</dt>
              <dd>
                Passwords, financial info, government IDs, health records, and private messages show up in red with an
                asterisk. Those are the leaks you actually need to do something about right away, so you can filter the
                list just for them.
              </dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Accounts</dt>
              <dd>
                The number of account records in a breach based on HIBP's count. Because the same person can show up in
                dozens of breaches, the total at the top of the homepage adds up every exposed record across all of
                them.
              </dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>HIBP listing interval</dt>
              <dd>
                The gap between the recorded breach date and when HIBP added it to the database. It doesn't tell you
                when the breach was actually discovered or publicly disclosed.
              </dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>How it works</h2>
          <dl class="how">
            <div>
              <dt>Static pages</dt>
              <dd>
                Every case file is built ahead of time from HIBP's public breach feed and served as static HTML.
                There's no app server or database running behind the site.
              </dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>No tracking</dt>
              <dd>
                I didn't put any analytics, cookies, or tracking scripts on here, and TRACED doesn't log or profile
                visitors. The hosting provider can still keep standard server request logs on their end.
              </dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Password check</dt>
              <dd>
                When you test a password, your browser hashes it locally and only sends the first 5 characters of that
                hash to Pwned Passwords. The actual password never leaves your device.
              </dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>Data sources</h2>
          <p>
            Everything here comes from Troy Hunt's Have I Been Pwned, a public breach database he's been running since
            2013.
          </p>

          <div class="sources">
            <article class="source">
              <h3>Have I Been Pwned</h3>
              <p>
                The main public breach list. It includes dates, affected account counts, exposed data types, and
                whether HIBP verified the breach. The public feed is free to read and doesn't need an API key.
              </p>
              <ul class="endpoints data">
                <li>GET haveibeenpwned.com/api/v3/breaches</li>
                <li>GET haveibeenpwned.com/api/v3/breach/{'{name}'}</li>
              </ul>
              <a href="https://haveibeenpwned.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">haveibeenpwned.com<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>
            </article>

            <article class="source">
              <h3>Pwned Passwords</h3>
              <p>
                Hundreds of millions of real passwords exposed in past breaches. The password checker uses k-anonymity
                when it calls this API, so your password and its full hash never leave your browser.
              </p>
              <ul class="endpoints data">
                <li>GET api.pwnedpasswords.com/range/{'{prefix}'}</li>
              </ul>
              <a href="https://haveibeenpwned.com/Passwords" target="_blank" rel="noopener">haveibeenpwned.com/Passwords<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>
            </article>
          </div>

          <p class="muted">
            All of the breach data belongs to Troy Hunt and Have I Been Pwned. TRACED is an unofficial, independent
            frontend and isn't affiliated with or endorsed by HIBP.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>Built with</h2>
          <p>
            The site runs on Astro 7 for static pages, React 19 for search and the password checker, Tailwind CSS v4
            and
            <a href="https://starwind.dev" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Starwind UI<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>
            for styling and components, and Cloudflare Pages for hosting. The fonts are Martian Mono, Doto, and
            Atkinson Hyperlegible, all self-hosted on this domain.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .byline {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .byline-photo {
    width: 3rem;
    height: 3rem;
    border-radius: 50%;
    object-fit: cover;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .how {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.25rem;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .how dt {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-stretch: var(--display-stretch);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .how dd {
    margin: 0.15rem 0 0;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .sources {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }

  .source {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.6rem;
  }

  .source p {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .endpoints {
    display: grid;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    word-break: break-all;
  }

  .endpoints li {
    padding: 0.45rem 0.75rem;
  }

  .endpoints li:nth-child(odd) {
    background: var(--bar);
  }
</style>
